summary:
  Today's radiology news highlights significant advancements in research linking lifestyle factors like gaming and diet to health outcomes, alongside updates on medical practice and administrative news. Several articles point towards potential modifiable risk factors for chronic diseases like diabetes and Alzheimer's, influencing future clinical approaches.

top_stories[8]{title,source,url,category,importance,ai_summary}:
  Title: Researchers found a tipping point for video gaming and health
  Source: ScienceDaily Health
  URL: https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/01/260116035311.htm
  category: research
  importance: 95
  ai_summary: "Moderate gaming is harmless, but over 10 hours/week links to worse diets, weight, and sleep, suggesting balance is key for young people's health."
  Title: Statins may help almost everyone with type 2 diabetes live longer
  Source: ScienceDaily Health
  URL: https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/01/260115022812.htm
  category: research
  importance: 90
  ai_summary: "A UK study found statin use linked to fewer deaths and cardiac events in all risk levels of type 2 diabetes, challenging previous assumptions about who should receive preventive therapy."
  Title: One protein may decide whether brain chemistry heals or harms
  Source: ScienceDaily Health
  URL: https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/01/260115022811.htm
  category: research
  importance: 85
  ai_summary: "Tryptophan, crucial for brain chemistry, can become harmful in aging or neurological disease, potentially leading to memory loss, mood changes, and sleep problems."
  Title: Vitamin A may be helping cancer hide from the immune system
  Source: ScienceDaily Health
  URL: https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/01/260115022808.htm
  category: research
  importance: 80
  ai_summary: "A vitamin A byproduct disarms the immune system, allowing tumors to evade attack. A new drug targeting this pathway shows promise in boosting immune responses and slowing cancer growth."
  Title: This after-meal blood sugar spike may raise Alzheimer’s risk
  Source: ScienceDaily Health
  URL: https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/01/260115022756.htm
  category: research
  importance: 75
  ai_summary: "High post-meal blood sugar is linked to a greater risk of Alzheimer's disease, even without visible brain damage, suggesting management of this spike could be a key strategy for reducing dementia risk."
